CAT = "auforkliftLicence"; SEG = "LIC"
Q = [
("Which high risk work licence class covers a counterbalance forklift with a mast and fork arms?",
 "LF", ["LO", "WP", "CN"],
 "Class LF covers forklift trucks with a mast and an elevating load carriage fitted with fork arms or another attachment. LO is the separate class for order-picking forklift trucks."),
("Which of these forklifts needs an LO licence rather than an LF licence?",
 "An order-picker that lifts its operator", ["A gas counterbalance with a side shift", "A battery reach truck with fork arms", "A diesel counterbalance with a jib fitted"],
 "An order-picking forklift has its controls built into the platform that rises with the load carriage, so it has its own class, LO. Reach trucks and counterbalance trucks, with or without attachments, are LF."),
("What is the minimum age to hold a high risk work licence?",
 "18 years", ["16 years", "17 years", "21 years"],
 "Under the WHS Regulations an applicant for a high risk work licence must be at least 18, so a younger worker cannot be issued the licence."),
("How long is a high risk work licence usually valid before it must be renewed?",
 "5 years", ["1 year", "3 years", "10 years"],
 "High risk work licences are issued for 5 years. Renew before the expiry date, as you cannot do high risk work on an expired licence. Once it is too long past expiry (12 months in NSW, for example) you must be trained and reassessed; the cut-off varies by state."),
("After passing your LF assessment, how long do you generally have to lodge your licence application?",
 "60 days", ["14 days", "30 days", "12 months"],
 "The WHS Regulations allow 60 days from the assessment to apply. Miss that window and you usually have to sit the assessment again; check your regulator's exact rule."),
("Who issues a forklift high risk work licence?",
 "The state or territory WHS regulator", ["Safe Work Australia directly", "The registered training organisation", "Your employer's safety officer"],
 "The WHS regulator in your state or territory issues the licence. Safe Work Australia develops national policy and assessment materials but does not issue licences, and the training organisation only assesses you."),
("You hold an LF licence from one state and take a job in another. Can you keep driving?",
 "Yes, other states recognise it", ["No, you must be reassessed there", "Only once you transfer it", "Only for the first 60 days"],
 "A current high risk work licence issued under WHS laws is recognised in every other state and territory. You still need site induction and familiarisation on the trucks at the new workplace."),
("When may a person without an LF licence operate a forklift at work?",
 "While training, under supervision", ["Only on private property, off road", "Only with no load fitted", "Only below 10 km/h"],
 "The licence rules allow a person enrolled in training for the licence to operate under the supervision of a licence holder. Private property, empty forks or low speed are not exemptions."),
("What must you do with your licence while working as a forklift operator?",
 "Keep it available for inspection", ["Leave it with your employer", "Display it inside the truck's cab", "Post it to the regulator yearly"],
 "A licence holder must keep the licence available so a WHS inspector can see it on request. Handing it to the employer to hold or fixing it to the truck does not meet that duty."),
("Under WHS laws, who has the primary duty to ensure plant at a workplace is safe?",
 "The business, or PCBU", ["The licensed operator", "The plant's manufacturer", "The safety representative"],
 "The person conducting a business or undertaking (PCBU) has the primary duty of care for plant and systems of work. Operators and manufacturers have their own duties, but the primary duty sits with the PCBU."),
("Which best describes a forklift operator's duty as a worker under WHS laws?",
 "Take reasonable care for all", ["Fix every defect on the truck", "Set the site's speed limits", "Supply their own safety gear"],
 "Workers must take reasonable care for their own health and safety and that of others, and follow reasonable instructions and procedures. Repairs, speed limits and PPE supply are for the PCBU and competent people."),
("Which control in the hierarchy of control is the most effective?",
 "Elimination", ["Personal protective equipment", "Administrative controls", "Engineering controls"],
 "The hierarchy runs elimination, substitution, isolation, engineering, administrative, then PPE. Removing the hazard altogether is the most effective; PPE is the least."),
("Fixed barriers that separate pedestrian walkways from forklift aisles are which type of control?",
 "Isolation", ["Administrative", "Substitution", "Personal protective"],
 "Isolating the hazard puts distance or a physical barrier between people and the forklift. Rules and signs alone would be administrative controls."),
("A roster that keeps pedestrians out of the loading dock while forklifts work is which type of control?",
 "Administrative", ["Isolation", "Engineering", "Substitution"],
 "A roster or procedure relies on people following it, so it is an administrative control. A physical barrier would be isolation and is more reliable."),
("Your pre-start check finds a fault that affects safety. What should you do?",
 "Tag it out and report it", ["Use it carefully till shift end", "Fix it yourself if you can", "Note it and keep working"],
 "A truck with a safety defect must be isolated, tagged out of service and reported to an authorised person. Only a competent person should repair it."),
("What is the purpose of an out of service tag on a forklift?",
 "Warns others not to use it", ["Records the last service date", "Shows who owns the truck", "Lists the truck's capacity"],
 "The tag tells everyone the truck is unsafe and must not be used until it is repaired and cleared. Service dates and capacity are found in the logbook and on the data plate."),
("Under most tag out procedures, who may remove a personal danger tag?",
 "The person who fitted it", ["Any licensed operator", "The next shift leader", "Whoever needs the truck next"],
 "A personal danger tag is removed by the person who placed it, or under a strict site procedure if that person cannot be found. Removing someone else's tag can put them in danger."),
("Where should you record the result of your pre-start check?",
 "In the truck's logbook", ["On a note left on the seat", "In your own phone notes", "On the truck's data plate"],
 "Pre-start results go in the logbook or checklist for that truck, so faults are tracked and the next operator can see them. Loose notes get lost and the data plate must never be altered."),
("Which Australian Standard series covers powered industrial trucks such as forklifts?",
 "AS 2359", ["AS 1418", "AS 4084", "AS 1657"],
 "AS 2359 covers powered industrial trucks, and the assessment truck must comply with it. AS 1418 covers cranes, AS 4084 steel storage racking and AS 1657 fixed platforms and ladders."),
("Which Australian Standard covers the steel storage racking forklifts load and unload?",
 "AS 4084", ["AS 2359", "AS 1418", "AS 3000"],
 "AS 4084 is the steel storage racking standard, which covers rack load signs and damage inspection. AS 2359 is for the trucks themselves and AS 3000 is the wiring rules."),
("You are told to drive a forklift with failed brakes to finish a job. What should you do?",
 "Stop and raise your concern", ["Drive it, then report it", "Do it but go slower", "Ask a co-worker to drive it instead"],
 "Under WHS laws a worker can cease unsafe work where there is a serious risk from immediate exposure, and must report it. Handing the risk to a co-worker does not remove it."),
("Before letting a worker drive a forklift, what must the PCBU see?",
 "Evidence of their LF licence", ["Their car driver licence", "A doctor's eyesight report", "The regulator's written consent"],
 "A PCBU must not direct or allow a worker to do high risk work without seeing evidence of the licence, or that they are a supervised trainee. A car licence is not the same thing."),
("Your LF licence card is lost. What should you do?",
 "Apply for a replacement", ["Resit the full assessment", "Ask your trainer for a copy", "Keep working, it's on file"],
 "Apply to your state or territory regulator for a replacement licence. The licence is still valid, but you must be able to produce it, and trainers cannot issue copies."),
("Who conducts the assessment for a forklift high risk work licence?",
 "An accredited assessor", ["Your site supervisor", "Any licensed operator", "A WHS inspector on site"],
 "The licence assessment is done by an assessor accredited to assess that class, using the nationally endorsed assessment materials. A supervisor or another operator cannot sign you off."),
("What does the LF licence assessment include?",
 "Knowledge and practical tests", ["A practical test only", "A written test only", "A logbook of 100 supervised hours"],
 "Candidates must pass a knowledge assessment, which includes load calculations, and a practical assessment on a real forklift. There is no minimum logbook hours requirement."),
("Can the practical part of the LF assessment be done on a forklift simulator?",
 "No, a real truck is needed", ["Yes, for the whole practical", "Yes, for the driving section", "Only with the regulator's consent"],
 "The TLILIC0003 assessment requirements say simulators must not be used. The practical is done on a real forklift that complies with AS 2359, in a real or realistic workplace."),
("Which of these machines is outside the LF licence class?",
 "A telehandler fitted with forks", ["An LPG counterbalance forklift", "A battery reach truck", "A diesel forklift fitted with a jib"],
 "A telehandler with forks or a bucket does not need an LF licence, although the worker must still be trained. Reach trucks and counterbalance trucks, with or without attachments, are LF."),
("Which national body developed the high risk work licence assessment materials?",
 "Safe Work Australia", ["Standards Australia", "The training organisation", "The truck's manufacturer"],
 "Safe Work Australia develops the national high risk work licence policy and assessment instruments that accredited assessors use. Standards Australia publishes standards such as AS 2359."),
]
